<title>BELGIUM: EU cuts duty for central/eastern European cereals.</title>
<headline>EU cuts duty for central/eastern European cereals.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-01-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Union's cereals management committee has voted to allow imports of cereals from central and eastern European countries at reduced rates of duty, an EU official said on Friday.</p>
<p>&quot;The committee voted unanimously for the regulation benefiting the so-called PECOS countries,&quot; the official said.</p>
<p>The agreement allows for two identical provisions covering the periods from January 1 to June 30 and from July 1 to December 31 (1997).</p>
<p>All imports will benefit from an 80 percent reduction in import duties with the exception of millet, which is reduced by 65 European currency units (Ecus) per tonne.</p>
<p>The half-year quantities which will be subject to the reduced duties are (all in tonnes) -</p>
<p>HUNGARY</p>
<p> durum or common wheat		  116,000</p>
<p> millet					 4,500</p>
<p>CZECH REPUBLIC</p>
<p> malting barley			    13,700</p>
<p> wheat flour				  6,750</p>
<p> unroasted malt (excluding wheat)    18,200</p>
<p>SLOVAKIA</p>
<p> malting barley			     6,800</p>
<p> wheat flour				  6,750</p>
<p> unroasted malt (excluding wheat)     7,230</p>
<p>POLAND</p>
<p> buckwheat				    2,175</p>
<p>BULGARIA</p>
<p> common wheat				 1,256</p>
<p> millet					   798</p>
<p>ROMANIA</p>
<p> common wheat				11,420</p>
